{"name":"Gold of Triumph","hex":"#e2b051","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"triumph","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A conclusive success following an effort, conflict, or confrontation of obstacles; victory; conquest.","A magnificent and imposing ceremonial performed in honor of a victor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To celebrate victory with pomp; to rejoice over success; to exult in an advantage gained; to exhibit exultation.","To prevail over rivals, challenges, or difficulties."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/triumph"}],"entries":[{"title":"Triumph (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Triumph is a Canadian rock band formed in 1975 that was popular during the late 1970s and the 1980s, building on their reputation and success as a live band. Between its 16 albums and DVDs, the band has received 18 gold and nine platinum awards in Canada and the United States. They were nominated for multiple Juno Awards, including the \"Group of the Year Award\" in 1979, 1985, 1986 and 1987. They were inducted into the Canadian Music Industry Hall of Fame in 2007, the Canadian Music Hall of Fame in 2008 and Canada's Walk of Fame in 2019."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Triumph_at_sweden_rock%2C_2008.JPG/330px-Triumph_at_sweden_rock%2C_2008.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triumph_(band)","wikidata":"Q1849053"},{"title":"Triumph (Triumph album)","blocks":[{"text":"Triumph is the debut studio album by Canadian rock band Triumph, released in 1976. The album was remastered and re-released with a new cover and name in 1995 titled In the Beginning."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/20/Triumph_self-titled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triumph_(Triumph_album)","wikidata":"Q1774727"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fc/In_the_Beginning_%28Triumph_album%29_cover_art.jpg/250px-In_the_Beginning_%28Triumph_album%29_cover_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fc/In_the_Beginning_%28Triumph_album%29_cover_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fc/In_the_Beginning_%28Triumph_album%29_cover_art.jpg/250px-In_the_Beginning_%28Triumph_album%29_cover_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm